@startuml
!pragma layout smetana
!include _uml-style.puml
title Session phases and outcomes — UML state machine
hide empty description
hide stereotype

state "Selecting" as Selecting
state "Supervising" as Supervising
state "Cleaning up and\ndeciding the outcome" as Settling
state "Writing the result" as Writing
state "Cancelled" as Cancelled <<outcome>>
state "Refused" as Refused <<outcome>>
state "No result" as Missing <<outcome>>
state "Failed" as Failed <<outcome>>
state "Succeeded" as Succeeded <<outcome>>

[*] --> Selecting
Selecting --> Supervising : worker cleanup confirmed;\ncommand checked and launched
Selecting --> Settling : refusal, timeout, failure\nor cancellation before launch
Supervising --> Settling : publication and job ending,\nnatural exit, failure or cancellation
Settling --> Cancelled : [cancellation\nselected]
Settling --> Refused : [refusal\nis primary]
Settling --> Missing : [missing result\nis primary]
Settling --> Failed : [other failure\nis primary]
Settling -[#135E50]-> Writing : [valid result; no cancellation;\nreap, group absence and cleanup confirmed]
Writing -[#135E50]-> Succeeded : write and flush succeed
Writing --> Failed : write or flush fails

legend bottom
  Blue: phases of Ravel's work, not processes.
  Grey rounded boxes: outcomes; no further transition is shown.
  INT, TERM or HUP can cancel either phase and suppress any published value.
  Cleanup is attempted on every path; attempts do not imply success.
  Ravel keeps one primary outcome and reports other failures beside it.
  No result means absent publication, even if the managed process exited 0.
  An empty publication is a value; a later nonzero managed exit does not invalidate it.
  Green: the only route to delivering a result successfully.
  A failed write may leave a prefix on stdout.
  This view groups operations; it is not the full failure-precedence algorithm.
endlegend
@enduml
